  "script": {
    "segments": [
      {
        "narration": "A fire under this town has burned since 1962. Sixty years on, nobody has found a way to put it out.",
        "visual_query": "pennsylvania forest smoking ground vents",
        "visual_fallback": "smoking ground"
      },
      {
        "narration": "Centralia was a mining borough in Pennsylvania, sitting straight on top of worked-out anthracite seams.",
        "visual_query": "appalachian coal mining town street",
        "visual_fallback": "mining town"
      },
      {
        "narration": "On the twenty-seventh of May, 1962, the council burned the town dump. The flames found a coal seam through an unsealed pit.",
        "visual_query": "burning landfill pit rural pennsylvania",
        "visual_fallback": "burning pit"
      },
      {
        "narration": "Then gardens and cellars started venting smoke. In 1981 the ground swallowed twelve-year-old Todd Domboski, 46 metres down. His cousin hauled him out.",
        "visual_query": "collapsed sinkhole suburban back yard",
        "visual_fallback": "sinkhole ground"
      },
      {
        "narration": "The state condemned every building in the borough in 1992. At the current rate, the seam holds enough coal to burn for another 250 years.",
        "visual_query": "abandoned cracked road pennsylvania hills",
        "visual_fallback": "cracked road"
      }
    ],
